<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Tunisia</h1><p class="st-meta">1956 CE – 1957 CE · capital Tunis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tunisia's brief monarchy after independence, in which the last Husainid bey reigned as king while Habib Bourguiba held power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-protectorate-of-tunisia">French protectorate of Tunisia</a> ended in March 1956, the bey <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-amin-bey">Muhammad al-Amin</a>, known as Lamine Bey, became head of an independent kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habib-bourguiba">Habib Bourguiba</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-destour">Neo-Destour</a> party, became prime minister after elections to a constituent assembly.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Real power lay with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habib-bourguiba">Bourguiba</a>, who sidelined the monarchy and the rival Youssefist faction. In August 1956 the government issued the Code of Personal Status, abolishing polygamy and reforming divorce, among the most progressive family laws in the Arab world. Religious courts were unified and lands of religious endowments reformed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 25 July 1957 the constituent assembly abolished the monarchy and proclaimed a republic, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habib-bourguiba">Bourguiba</a> as president. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-amin-bey">Lamine Bey</a> was placed under house arrest, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beylik-of-tunis">Husainid dynasty</a>, which had ruled since 1705.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-tunisia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Tunisia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file: · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Beylik_of_Tunis_(1831%E2%80%931881)_and_Tunisia_(1881%E2%80%931959).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1956 CE – 1957 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tunis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad al-Amin Bey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Tunisia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tunisian Kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
